<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ausgleich, Compromise of 1867, 1867 Compromise</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867, or Ausgleich, was the agreement between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> that created the dual monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. Hungary received its own parliament and government, while foreign affairs, the army and joint finances were shared. It founded the Austro-Hungarian Empire and shaped its governance until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867.jpg" alt="Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kpalion / modified by AndreasPraefcke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cisleithanien_Transleithanien.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Compromise of 1867</b> recognised the Kingdom of Hungary as an equal partner with its own government and parliament, joined to the remaining lands by the person of the monarch and by common ministries for foreign affairs, war and joint finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Ausgleich</b>, or Compromise, of 1867 was the product of negotiations between Emperor Franz Joseph I and Hungarian liberal leaders, above all Ferenc Deák and Count Gyula Andrássy, who recognised that a weakened Austrian Empire needed Hungarian cooperation and that Hungary could extract far-reaching autonomy from a dynasty…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1849 Transylvania was again a separate crown land, until the <b>Compromise of 1867</b> merged it into the Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918).</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-transylvania">Grand Principality of Transylvania</a></li><li><blockquote>Neo-absolutist rule followed until the <b>Compromise of 1867</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-state-1849">Hungarian State (1849)</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat by Prussia in 1866 forced the <b>Compromise of 1867</b>, which created Austria-Hungary and the self-governing Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918).</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1526-1867">Kingdom of Hungary (1526-1867)</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Compromise of 1867</b> restored Hungary's constitution and parliament, with the emperor crowned king, and created Austria-Hungary, sharing only the monarch, foreign policy, the army and common finances.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1867-1918">Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918)</a></li></ul></section>
